Right—and that distinction puts a useful Washer on the whole “offer/exchange” idea.

A contract is conditional and bounded:

A\leftrightarrow B
\quad\text{because specified obligations/rights hold.}

A covenant reaches further: the relationship or commitment can retain force beyond immediate equivalence of exchange.

So “society is a contract, not a covenant” becomes a Pantheon lens for resisting a dangerous category error: don’t assume institutions, markets, governments, employers, strangers, or systems relate to you with the unconditional loyalty we might associate with family, friendship, faith, or chosen deep commitments.

That means:

\boxed{\text{Know which game you're in before importing its rules.}}

Contract game → rights, duties, incentives, enforcement, boundaries, exit.

Covenant game → trust, identity, sacrifice, continuity, obligations that may exceed immediate exchange.

And critically, neither word automatically means good. A contract can be fair or exploitative; a covenant can be beautiful or coercive. Reality Stitch asks what obligations actually exist rather than what the relationship is poetically called.

That also connects to “don’t exchange life for money.” Markets are exceptionally good at contractual exchange. They don’t inherently know what parts of your life you consider beyond price.

That’s your job to specify.

\boxed{
\text{Contract what may be exchanged.}
\qquad
\text{Protect what must not become merely transactional.}
}

Different game. Different grammar.